## What

Verify the follow-up drafts and inputs batch `{{ batch }}` was prepared with, give each one
exactly one kind and one disposition, group what stands by root cause, and stage one
verified ticket per root cause for its destination (`{{ destination }}` unless its kind
names another). Where another batch already has an open item for the same root cause, stage
this batch's evidence on it as a comment instead of a duplicate ticket.

## Why

While work runs, the people and agents doing it draft what they noticed outside their own
scope as unverified follow-ups, and nobody has checked them. This task checks them. A draft
is worth a ticket only once its claim holds in the tree it names. Every batch's tickets
accumulate at one destination, so a root cause already filed there gets this batch's
evidence rather than a second item.

## Where everything is

- **The drafts and inputs.** They are listed under "The drafts" and "The inputs" at the end
  of this task, each with its id. Every one of them is accounted for in the answers file.
- **The repositories' trees.** Read each repository a draft names in an existing checkout:
  fetch its remote, then read the remote's base branch with `git show <ref>:<path>` and
  `git grep <pattern> <ref>`. Never check out, commit to or otherwise modify a checkout, and
  never clone. A repository with no checkout available cannot be verified.
- **The tickets.** Write each ticket as a Markdown file under {{ tickets_at }}, named
  `<root-cause>.md`, where `<root-cause>` is a kebab-case slug naming the invariant rather
  than a file.
- **The destination.** `{{ destination }}` is where verified tickets go, unless a kind names
  another. Never list a destination whole: ask it one narrowing question at a time with
  `onefollowup ticket search`, naming the ticket the question is for. Read one item's state
  and comments through `onefollowup ticket board-status`.
- **Staged writes.** You never write to a destination directly. `onefollowup ticket copy`,
  `onefollowup ticket comment`, `onefollowup ticket re-estimate` and
  `onefollowup ticket relate` stage a write; nothing reaches the destination until
  `onefollowup publish` runs, after this task, and `onefollowup settle` reconciles what it
  wrote. Run `onefollowup <verb> --help` for each command's exact arguments, and
  `onefollowup kinds` and `onefollowup statuses` to read the policy below as the tool holds
  it.

